abstract:
PCA asks "which directions carry the most variance?" t-SNE asks a completely different question: "can I place these points on a page so that everyone's neighbours stay their neighbours?" It converts distances into probabilities in both spaces and minimises the KL divergence between them. We derive $P$ and $Q$, explain perplexity as an effective neighbour count, show why the Student-t distribution solves the crowding problem, and — most importantly — lay out the four ways t-SNE plots are routinely misread.}
